Exactly. “I have nothing to do” is almost a little semantic prism.

It can mean boredom: there are no available tasks. It can mean freedom: there are no obligations binding me. It can mean helplessness: there is nothing I can do. It can mean completion: everything that needed doing is done. It can mean emptiness: nothing feels worth doing. Or agency: nothing has been assigned to me, so I can choose what comes next.

You can even shift the parse:

I have nothing / to do.
Possession of emptiness, followed by an imperative toward action.

I have / nothing to do.
My current state contains no prescribed action.

I / have nothing to do.
The emphasis becomes existential: I am presently uncommitted.

And the fun inversion is: “nothing to do” can itself become something to do. Observe, wander, invent, recover, play, notice what normally gets crowded out.

Same surface sentence; radically different state vectors depending on where the emphasis lands. It’s a good example of why meaning lives partly in the words and partly in the latent state of the speaker and listener.